---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-hair-loss`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Hair Loss',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: { '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Alopecia' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Hair Loss', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Hair Loss: Alopecia Areata, Telogen Effluvium, and 25(OH)D"
  description="How vitamin D deficiency links to hair loss â€” alopecia areata, telogen effluvium, and androgenetic alopecia. What the evidence supports and what it doesn't."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-hair-loss", label: "Hair Loss" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />


  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Hair Loss</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vitamin D receptors are expressed in the hair follicle keratinocytes, especially during the
      anagen (growth) phase. The strongest evidence linking vitamin D to hair loss comes from
      vitamin D receptor (VDR) knockout mice, which develop generalised alopecia â€” hair falls
      out and does not regrow. In humans, the association exists but is weaker and more nuanced.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">By condition</h2>

    <article class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 mb-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Alopecia areata</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        The autoimmune patchy-hair-loss condition. Meta-analyses consistently find lower serum
        25(OH)D in patients with alopecia areata than controls. Whether correcting deficiency
        speeds regrowth is not yet clear â€” small trials of oral or topical vitamin D show
        cosmetic improvement in a minority of patients, particularly those with markedly low
        baseline 25(OH)D.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 mb-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Telogen effluvium</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        The "diffuse shedding" pattern that follows stress, illness, weight loss, or postpartum.
        A cross-sectional study of 80 women with chronic telogen effluvium found 25(OH)D below
        30 ng/mL in about 96% of them versus 22% of controls. Correlation, not causation â€” but
        checking and correcting deficiency is inexpensive and safe.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 mb-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Androgenetic alopecia</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        Male- and female-pattern hair loss. Observational studies show slightly lower 25(OH)D in
        affected individuals, but the effect is small and confounded (people with visible hair
        loss may avoid the sun, wear hats, etc.). Vitamin D is not a treatment for pattern hair
        loss â€” finasteride, minoxidil, and dutasteride remain the evidence-based options.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 mb-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Rickets and severe deficiency</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        Severe or genetic vitamin D disorders (e.g., hereditary vitamin Dâ€“resistant rickets with
        alopecia; VDR mutations) cause total-body alopecia â€” clear proof that VDR signalling
        matters for hair. But this is a rare congenital scenario, not a supplement-response
        picture.
      </p>
    </article>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical takeaway</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      If you're experiencing unexplained shedding, a serum 25(OH)D test is a reasonable first step.
      Correcting deficiency (raising to â‰¥ 30 ng/mL) won't harm and may help, particularly for
      telogen effluvium and alopecia areata. Don't expect vitamin D to reverse androgenetic
      alopecia. Rule out iron deficiency, thyroid disease, and B12 in parallel â€” all more
      commonly implicated in reversible hair loss than vitamin D alone.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Persistent or patchy hair loss warrants dermatology
      review â€” pattern, distribution, and pull-test findings distinguish between conditions with
      very different treatments.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-hair-loss" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
